t in the  courts and the meconium to where the  chords actually seem to be more  sensitive to oxycodone than the meconium  and the meconium was picking up a number  of the medications that are administered  directly to the newborn such as codeine  morphine lorazepam and phenobarbital so  this actually really confounds the  interpretation of the results because  you don't know for sure whether the baby  received these drugs and that explains  the result or if the mom received the  drugs in addition to baby receiving drug  here  from Vanderbilt University they  published a parrot result study for  court and meconium where they found in  217 newborns that there were 114  discordant results and so what I want to  highlight is that sometimes the court is  positive  sometimes the meconium is positive  sometimes both are positive but for  different analytes and so the specimens  even though they both are thought to  reflect drug use during the last  trimester do have differences in terms  of sensitivity and at the laboratory  methods are going to contribute to that  as well so in the case of Vanderbilt  they concluded that the court collection  was logistically preferred but that they  did not choose to switch and stuck with  their meconium which they were more  comfortable with here a study that was  recently published from Ohio State  University showed very good negative  agreement and mixed positive agreement  what they what I think was most notable  is that they found their clinical  sensitivity for Naas was improved with  court but that there were still a number  of babies with Naas diagnoses for which  both meconium and cord results were  negative so in their in their case they  chose to to switch to court because of  the logistics that they also recognize  that not everything is going to be  detected and that we still have room for  improvement in monitoring these babies  so this risk of false negatives is the  biggest concern and so it's important to  consider the 