toring drug adherence during  pregnancy and lactation whereas meconium  and umbilical cord are the preferred  specimens although not perfect and they  are the preferred specimens for  evaluating drug adherence it with  buprenorphine and methadone as well as  for detecting use of non prescribed or  illicit drugs the drug testing results  have to be interpreted based on the  maternal and infant history as well as  strengths and limitations of the  associated specimens and the analytical  methods so thank you for your attention  thank you dr. McMillan for that  informative presentation we will now  start the live Q&amp;A portion of the  webinar if you have a question you'd  like to ask please do so now just click  on the green Q&amp;A button at the lower  left of the presentation window  type your question into the box that  appears on your screen  and click the send button well answer as  many of your questions as we have time  for so let's get started with our first  question  our first question asks is it  recommended that breast milk completely  - we routinely tested for drugs so there  are not any specific guidelines related  to testing breast milk there are  laboratories that offer breast milk  testing and if there's a good reason to  test the breast milk such as a serious  and suspected exposure it may be  appropriate  however breast milk represents only a  few hours since drug exposure for most  drugs and so it will really only reflect  very recent or daily cues of drugs  breast milk is also challenging and that  the concentrations of the drugs may be  different in the for milk kind milk and  other portions of a feeding so if one is  going to test breast milk the preferred  approach would be to collect an entire  feeding and mix that and then send that  in for testing relative to  interpretation as I mentioned earlier  you need to really think about how much  milk the infant consumes relative to the  infant's weight when looking at the  concentrations so interpretation is is  really challenging and I wouldn't really  recommend thank you for your answer our  second question asks when is chain of  custody required for drug testing so  chain of custody is a forensic or legal  document that authenticates every step  of the process from specimen collection  to destruction and so it's very  important to use a chain of custody if a  result is going to be admitted as  evidence in a criminal court system or  other forensic proceedings for most of  the testing  associated with detecting in utero  exposure forensic processing is not  required because the purpose of testing  you know that the whole reason is is  medically based to manage the patient  treat the patient provide the care  necessary for the mother and the infant  sometimes medical testing results are  utilized for sayed child custody  scenarios which are usually heard in a  civil court system and in my experience  chain of custody is not routinely  required as long as the policies and  procedures that the hospital EMA  laboratory have for specimen collection  handling and testing are followed thank  you  the next question asks should drugs  other than opioids be included in a drug  test intended for chronic pain patient  so opioids have gotten most of the  attention and that's likely because of  the opioid epidemic that we've seen in  our population in general which has now  led into the pregnant population and the  association of opioids with dnas however  other drugs are important to other drugs  can contribute to the the onset and  severity as well as duration of Naas and  so it is practical to test for other  drugs it's not feasible for most labs to  look for any possible drug drug and  there really aren't any guidelines to to  dictate the content of an assay so most  laboratories choose to offer panels that  include opioids sedatives like  benzodiazepines and barbiturates  stimulants like amphetamines and cocaine  and cannabis to cover commonly  controlled prescribe